  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Determine the value of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n}}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5},r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3\\,124}{3\\,125} \\\\  & =\\frac{781}{3\\,125} \\\\  & \u22480.250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places.",
